<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinando II de' Medici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a>–<a href="/years/1670">1670</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ferdinand II de' Medici · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">House of Medici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinando II de' Medici was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a> from 1621 to 1670. His long reign saw plague, economic decline and the indecisive War of Castro against the Barberini. A patron of science, he protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a>, his former tutor, after his condemnation in 1633, and with his brother Leopoldo he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accademia-del-cimento">Accademia del Cimento</a> in 1657, one of the first scientific societies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinando-ii-de-medici.jpg" alt="Ferdinando II de' Medici" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Justus Sustermans · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sustermans,_Justus_-_Official_portrait_of_Ferdinando_II_de%27_Medici_as_Grand_Duke_of_Tuscany.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinando was born on 14 July 1610, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-ii-de-medici">Cosimo II de' Medici</a> and Maria Maddalena of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. He was ten years old when his father died, and his mother and his paternal grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christina-of-lorraine">Christina of Lorraine</a> acted as joint regents. They arranged his marriage to Vittoria della Rovere, a granddaughter of the last Duke of Urbino, hoping to acquire the duchy, but they were too weak politically to secure it, and the Papal States conquered Urbino. In his seventeenth year Ferdinando toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. His personal rule began a year later, though Christina remained the power behind the throne until her death in 1636.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A plague swept through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1630 and killed a tenth of its people. Unlike the Tuscan nobility, Ferdinando and his brothers stayed in the city and went through it on foot to ease the suffering. Tuscany took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-castro">Wars of Castro</a>, its last military conflict under the Medici, and defeated the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii">Pope Urban VIII</a> in 1643. The treasury was so empty that the state could not pay interest on its bonds, and the rate was lowered by 0.75 per cent. The economy decayed to the point that barter became common in rural markets, the beginning of a long Tuscan decline.</p><p>Ferdinando was fascinated by new instruments and installed hygrometers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barometer">barometers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermometer">thermometers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescopes</a> in the Palazzo Pitti, and in 1654 he is reported to have invented a sealed-glass spirit thermometer, marked with 360 divisions. His brother Leopoldo established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accademia-del-cimento">Accademia del Cimento</a> in 1657 to bring scientists from across Tuscany to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and Ferdinando took an active part in it. Like his father, he was a patron and friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a>, who dedicated his Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems to him. He tried to keep the Holy See from holding a full hearing and kept Galileo in Florence until December 1632. In June 1633 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-inquisition">Roman Inquisition</a> convicted Galileo, and after his sentence was commuted to house arrest Ferdinando visited him at his villa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ferdinando died on 23 May 1670 of apoplexy and dropsy and was buried in the Basilica of San Lorenzo, the Medici crypt. His marriage to Vittoria was unhappy, but produced two children who reached adulthood: Cosimo, born in 1642, who succeeded him as Cosimo III, and Francesco Maria, born in 1660. He was remembered by his contemporaries as a man of culture and science. His 49-year rule saw the start of the economic decline that his successor Cosimo III made worse.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinando II de' Medici” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ferdinando II</b> joined the league against the Barberini in the War of Castro in the 1640s, but after the mid-seventeenth century the grand dukes avoided war, and the state was largely demilitarised, especially under Peter Leopold, who reduced the army sharply.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1649" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4820 613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ferdinando II de' Medici"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1649">What was here in 1649? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
